    "outcome": "A consumer tests an unsaved rewrite with synthetic request input, verifies native route captures and the transformed target without outbound calls or writes, commits a separate SQLite rewrite revision, observes YARP-confirmed activation and forwards a real HTTP request to a local backend. The embedded rewrite editor supports creation, edits, enable/disable, shared destinations, path transforms, advanced typed configuration JSON, testing, conflict protection, deletion and activation retry. JSON is the agreed developer interface for advanced settings. It shows the complete current draft including the first path transform. Form fields and valid JSON edits synchronize immediately in both directions, while invalid JSON is preserved. Missing transform kinds in advanced JSON return HTTP 400 with the draft intact and leave saved and active revisions unchanged. The path control replaces the first path transform without duplicating it on submission. A per-response CSP nonce authorizes only the embedded editor script. Contextual YARP documentation links open in a new tab and explain that native YARP examples use a different JSON structure. Draft testing includes saved managed redirects before rewrites and checks both revisions. The administration top bar tests a GET URL against saved rules using TestSavedAsync and shows the winning redirect or rewrite, target and edit link, with explicit no-match, reserved-path and saved-versus-active results. Rule evaluation lists redirect and rewrite names and paths, concrete mismatch reasons for path/regex, host/port, method, header and query conditions, and explicit skips for disabled rules or redirect precedence. Native routing distinguishes matching rewrites that lose on priority or specificity. These diagnostics describe the entered URL and are also shown in the rewrite draft editor. A path starting with / inherits the proxy request's scheme, host and port; full HTTP(S) URLs keep their own origin. The resolved request URL is displayed, and no administration path or PathBase is prepended. Host the proxy at the origin root with an empty PathBase. Subdirectory hosting is unsupported because URL/draft preview does not model the mount prefix; support is deferred until a concrete use case requires it. Native YARP APIs/configuration satisfy the requirement for rewrites from appsettings and other sources; no additional NewHeap source integration or external-source preview is planned. These sources, host customizations, upstream responses and policy enforcement intentionally remain outside preview. Regex with capture groups satisfies prefix and route-template redirect requirements; separate match modes are not planned. MaximumChainDepth defaults to 2 and is configurable through NewHeapProxy:Limits. The runtime and tester follow the concrete local managed-rule chain without outbound calls, count each redirect or rewrite, and refuse a further step with HTTP 508 or a safe test failure. Both carry transformed general and content headers into subsequent matches and preview output, preserving multiple values and header replacement/removal without reading the real body. Executable evidence verifies content-header preview and intact body forwarding. Only the original scheme/host/port within PathBase is followed; external destinations, aliases, host endpoints and backend responses are outside the analysis. SQLite is implemented; SQL Server and PostgreSQL remain explicit provider gaps. The optional MapProxyEndpoints call before UseNewHeapProxy exposes a server-to-server Basic API under /newheap-proxy/api; without the call it stays unavailable. It reuses host administrator credentials and the IP allowlist without issuing cookies, creating login records or consuming browser login attempts. Bad API credentials have their own failure budget. HTTPS is required outside Development and Origin-bearing requests are denied. Before saving, the shared validator builds native YARP transforms and compiles regex route constraints, including disabled rules, without backend requests. Malformed route/transform patterns are rejected before changing configuration, revisions or audit. Invalid JSON, including missing transform kinds, returns 400 with a readable issue message and JSON field path; rejected requests do not save or audit. Full redirect/rewrite snapshot PUTs accept enum names and numeric values, including nested transforms, and use expected revisions; saved activation failures preserve their revision in the response and per-engine retry does not increment it. Status and saved/draft previews use existing services without outbound test traffic. ProxyApiSamplesTests demonstrates real SQLite updates, conflicts, preview and OpenAPI metadata; the standalone sample opts in only with ProxyApi=true. API changes commit their audit event atomically with the SQLite configuration: actor, effective IP, UTC time, correlation ID, engine, revisions and normalized before/after snapshots. GET /audit provides bounded paging and engine/time filters; records survive restart with no automatic expiration. Reads, previews, rejected saves and authentication create no change events. Activation requests are recorded as intent before retry. The schema-4 upgrade preserves existing configuration and login audit; audit insert failures roll back configuration saves.",
